Presidential elections were held in the [[Czech Republic]] in January 2013, the country's first direct election for the presidency.<ref name=signs>{{cite news |url= http://praguemonitor.com/2012/08/02/klaus-signs-direct-presidential-election-bill |title= Klaus signs direct presidential election bill |agency= [[ČTK]] |work= [[Prague Daily Monitor]] |date= 2 August 2012 |access-date= 21 September 2012 |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://archive.today/20130201040841/http://praguemonitor.com/2012/08/02/klaus-signs-direct-presidential-election-bill |archive-date= 1 February 2013 |df= dmy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.radio.cz/en/section/curraffrs/first-direct-presidential-election-set-for-january-11-2013|title=First direct presidential election set for January 11, 2013|last=Richter|first=Jan|date=2 October 2012|publisher=[[Czech Radio]]|access-date=27 January 2013}}</ref> No candidate received a majority of the votes in the first round on 11–12 January, so a second round [[runoff election]] was held on 25–26 January. Nine individuals secured enough signatures or support of parliamentarians to become official candidates for the office. [[Miloš Zeman]] of the [[Party of Civic Rights]] (SPOZ) and [[Karel Schwarzenberg]] of [[TOP 09]] qualified for the second round,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://zpravy.idnes.cz/prezidentske-volby-2013-0uh-/domaci.aspx?c=A130112_135527_domaci_jav|title=Zeman a Schwarzenberg se utkají o Hrad, Fischer uznal porážku|date=12 January 2013|work=[[Mladá fronta DNES]]|publisher=iDNES|language=cs|access-date=12 January 2013}}</ref> which was won by Zeman with 54.8% of the vote, compared to Schwarzenberg's 45.2%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://zpravy.idnes.cz/volba-prezidenta-0ej-/domaci.aspx?c=A130126_130102_domaci_jw|title=Rozhodnuto. Zeman vyhrál, nezmění to už ani velká města|date=26 January 2013|work=Mladá fronta DNES|publisher=iDNES|access-date=26 January 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-europe-21210495|title=Czech election: Milos Zeman wins presidential poll|date=26 January 2013|publisher=BBC|access-date=27 January 2013}}</ref> Zeman assumed office in March 2013 after being sworn in.

==Background==
[[File:TomásMasaryk.jpg|left|thumb|The [[Prague Castle]], official residence of the Czech President, behind the statue of [[Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk]], the first [[List of Presidents of Czechoslovakia|President of Czechoslovakia]]]]

After the dissolution of [[Czechoslovakia]] and the adoption of a [[Constitution of the Czech Republic|new constitution]] in 1992, the president was indirectly elected by a joint session of the [[Chamber of Deputies of the Czech Republic|Chamber of Deputies]] and the [[Senate of the Czech Republic]]. The possibility of a directly elected president was controversial because of concerns that it could weaken a government under the [[Prime Minister of the Czech Republic|prime minister]].<ref name=radio>{{cite news |url= http://www.radio.cz/en/section/curraffrs/czech-parliament-passes-direct-presidential-elections |title= Czech Parliament passes direct presidential elections |date= 2 February 2012 |access-date= 18 September 2012 |work= [[Radio Prague]] |first= Christian |last= Falvey}}</ref><ref name=fuhrer>{{cite news |url= http://www.ceskapozice.cz/en/news/politics-policy/will-czechs-elect-worthy-president-or-fuehrer |title= Will Czechs elect a worthy president, or a führer? |work= Czech Position |first= Petr |last= Nováček |date= 14 February 2012 |access-date= 21 September 2012 |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120220081306/http://www.ceskapozice.cz/en/news/politics-policy/will-czechs-elect-worthy-president-or-fuehrer |archive-date= 20 February 2012 |df= dmy-all }}</ref> The [[2008 Czech presidential election|2008 presidential election]], which narrowly reelected [[Václav Klaus]] after several attempts, however was criticized for the appearance of political deal-making and allegations of corruption.<ref name=fuhrer/><ref name=eight>{{cite news |url= http://www.radio.cz/en/section/curraffrs/klaus-celebrates-re-election-as-czech-president-after-vote-marked-by-controversy |title= Klaus celebrates re-election as Czech president after vote marked by controversy |work= Radio Prague |date= 18 February 2008 |access-date= 24 September 2012 |first= Ian |last= Willoughby}}</ref><ref name=svejnar/> Prime Minister [[Petr Nečas]] subsequently put the issue of a directly elected president in his three-party [[coalition agreement]] when he formed his government in 2010, in part because of demands by the [[TOP 09]] party, and the [[Public Affairs (political party)|Public Affairs]] and [[Mayors and Independents]] parties.<ref name=fuhrer/> Several outspoken opponents of the change however came from the Prime Minister's own [[Civic Democratic Party (Czech Republic)|Civic Democratic Party]].<ref name=opponents/>

In September 2011, an amendment was submitted to the Chamber of Deputies for a second official reading, during which the [[Communist Party of Bohemia and Moravia|Communist Party]] (KSČM) tried to reject the bill by sending it back into the review process, but the [[Czech Social Democratic Party]] (ČSSD), also part of the opposition, did not support the Communists' motion,<ref name=opponents>{{cite news |url= http://www.ceskapozice.cz/en/news/politics-policy/direct-presidential-elections-sorry-maybe-next-time |title= Direct presidential elections: Sorry, maybe next time |work= Czech Position |date= 26 September 2011 |access-date= 23 September 2012 |first= Petr |last= Nováček |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20111231191220/http://www.ceskapozice.cz/en/news/politics-policy/direct-presidential-elections-sorry-maybe-next-time |archive-date= 31 December 2011 |df= dmy-all }}</ref> and allowed the bill to go ahead with certain changes, including limits on presidential power and penal immunity.<ref name=january/> On 14 December 2011, the Chamber of Deputies passed the constitutional amendment for direct elections by a vote of 159 out of 192.<ref name=january>{{cite news |url= http://www.radio.cz/en/section/curraffrs/czech-mps-approve-direct-presidential-election |title= Czech MPs approve direct presidential election |work= Radio Prague |date= 14 December 2011 |access-date= 24 September 2012 |first= Jan |last= Richter}}</ref> This was then sent to the Senate, which passed the amendment on 8 February 2012 after five hours of debate<ref name=radio/> by a majority of 49 of 75.<ref name=senate>{{cite news |url= http://www.ceskenoviny.cz/news/zpravy/senate-passes-bill-introducing-direct-election-of-czech-president/752624 |title= Senate passes bill introducing direct election of Czech president |work= ČTK |date= 8 February 2012 |access-date= 24 September 2012}}</ref> The Chief Justice of the [[Constitutional Court of the Czech Republic|Constitutional Court]], [[Pavel Rychetský]], criticized the bill's method in which a [[List of methods of constitutional amendment#Czech Republic|constitutional amendment]] was in effect added, though without changing the original text of the constitution, and while leaving the election open to legal and constitutional challenges.<ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.ceskapozice.cz/en/news/politics-policy/constitutional-court-head-slams-direct-presidential-election-amendment |title= Constitutional Court head slams direct presidential election amendment |work= Czech Policy |first= Tom |last= Jones |date= 13 February 2012 |access-date= 24 September 2012 |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://archive.today/20130210014059/http://www.ceskapozice.cz/en/news/politics-policy/constitutional-court-head-slams-direct-presidential-election-amendment |archive-date= 10 February 2013 |df= dmy-all }}</ref>

In June 2012 an implementation bill for holding the election passed in the Chamber of Deputies, and in July in the Senate.<ref name=signs/> Though constitutional amendments do not require presidential approval, and cannot be vetoed,<ref name=senate/> President Václav Klaus did need to sign or veto the implementation bill; a refusal could have halted the constitutional changes.<ref name=signs/> Klaus opposed the measure, though saying it was a "fatal mistake"<ref name=radio/> as the country was not ready for such a move.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://presidentialactivism.com/2011/03/15/vaclav-klaus-against-direct-presidential-elections-in-czech-republic/ |title= Vaclav Klaus against direct presidential elections in Czech Republic |date= 15 March 2011 |access-date= 18 September 2012 |work= Presidential Activism}}</ref> He however signed the law on 1 August 2012.<ref name=signs/> The law was scheduled to take effect on 1 October 2012,{{Update after|2013|2|1}}<ref name=senate/> after which Senate President [[Milan Štěch]] was due to set a date for the election, following discussions with the Ministry of the Interior.{{update inline|date=February 2013}}<ref name=neurope/>

The two-day first round was on 11–12 January 2013. However, because no one secured an absolute majority, a run-off round was held on 25–26 January 2013.<ref name=neurope>{{cite news |url= http://www.neurope.eu/article/czech-republic-hold-first-direct-presidential-election-january |title= Czech Republic to hold first direct presidential election in January |date= 2 August 2012 |access-date= 18 September 2012 |work= New Europe |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130205232401/http://www.neurope.eu/article/czech-republic-hold-first-direct-presidential-election-january |archive-date= 5 February 2013 |url-status= dead }}</ref> Candidates were allowed to spend up to [[Kč]]40&nbsp;million in the first round and [[Kč]]10&nbsp;million in the second round. Each candidate had an election committee that manages campaign funding, which should be run through a special account. All anonymous campaigns contributions were banned.<ref name=signs/>

==Candidates==
In order to be a candidate, an individual needs to gather 50,000 signatures from citizens or the support of twenty [[Chamber of Deputies of the Parliament of the Czech Republic|deputies]] or ten [[Senate of the Parliament of the Czech Republic|senators]]. The candidates were bound to file their applications with the signatures sixty-six days before the election;<ref name=neurope/> following which the Interior Ministry verified a sampling of the signatures.<ref name=signs/>

The [[Civic Democratic Party (Czech Republic)|Civic Democratic Party]] (ODS) held [[2012 Civic Democratic Party presidential primaries|primary elections in July 2012]] to choose their candidate, selecting former president of the Senate [[Přemysl Sobotka]] over MEP [[Evžen Tošenovský]].<ref name=ods>{{cite news |url= http://praguemonitor.com/2012/07/18/civic-democrats-back-sobotka-president |title= Civic Democrats back Sobotka for president |agency= ČTK |work= Prague Daily Monitor |date= 18 July 2012 |access-date= 21 September 2012 |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://archive.today/20130201101425/http://praguemonitor.com/2012/07/18/civic-democrats-back-sobotka-president |archive-date= 1 February 2013 |df= dmy-all }}</ref> [[Party of Civic Rights – Zemanovci|SPOZ]], [[TOP 09]], and [[Czech Sovereignty of Social Democracy|Sovereignty]] have their party leaders running for the post. [[Jan Švejnar]], who [[2008 Czech presidential election|ran for the presidency in 2008]] against Václav Klaus, declined to run in order to support [[Jan Fischer (politician)|Jan Fischer]]'s candidacy.<ref name=svejnar>{{cite news |url= http://praguemonitor.com/2012/09/12/%C5%A1vejnar-says-he-will-not-seek-czech-presidency |title= Švejnar says he will not seek Czech presidency |agency= ČTK |date= 12 September 2012 |work= Prague Daily Monitor |access-date= 18 September 2012 |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://archive.today/20130131225148/http://praguemonitor.com/2012/09/12/%C5%A1vejnar-says-he-will-not-seek-czech-presidency |archive-date= 31 January 2013 |df= dmy-all }}</ref>

